 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that limit. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Replacement
+
+The cache maintains two queues:
+
+- **Probationary queue (A1)**: FIFO, bounded at 3 GiB (25 % of the 12 GiB budget). All newly completed sheets enter here.
+- **Protected queue (Am)**: LRU, holds the remaining capacity up to 9 GiB. A sheet moves here when a second request hits it while it is still in A1.
+
+**Admission**: a completed sheet enters at the tail of A1. If total cached JPEG data then exceeds 12 GiB, eviction runs immediately before the response is returned.
+
+**Promotion**: the first cache hit for a sheet in A1 moves that entry to the head of Am. Subsequent hits find it in Am and update its Am position normally.
+
+**Eviction order**: while total cached JPEG data exceeds 12 GiB, evict one entry at a time — if A1 exceeds its 3 GiB quota, evict the A1 tail; otherwise evict the Am tail. Repeat until the total is at or below 12 GiB.
+
+**Re-admission after eviction**: a sheet evicted from A1 before it was ever promoted re-enters A1 on the next render completion, not Am.
+
+### Open Questions
+
+The 3 GiB / 9 GiB split is an initial value. Pilot screening patterns may reveal that screenings are long enough to require a smaller A1 (reducing tail pressure on Am), or that editors revisit sheets quickly enough to tolerate a larger A1. The split is internal to the worker and can be changed without affecting the external interface or disk budget.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q: FIFO probationary queue (A1) + LRU protected queue (Am) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU follows recent work efficiently but a long sequential screening evicts the comparison-sheet working set before editors can revisit it. Frequency counters with aging retain repeatedly popular sheets but add per-entry counter maintenance and are slow to clear stale favorites when editors switch projects. 2Q separates first-use sheets (screening) from reused sheets (comparison), resisting scan eviction at the cost of an extra queue and a size-split parameter. The external interface, render queue, and disk budget are unaffected by the choice. |
